Interesting! The injector DID kick in: `read(3, ..., 10) = 10` — it clipped to 10 bytes. Then the next read should fail with EIO... but there's no further read(3) at all! The program printed the digest and exited 0.

Why? Because my loop: `got = 10 < 65536` → treated as normal EOF and broke out of the loop! The old ifstream-based code: `in.read(buf, 65536)` — the filebuf would keep reading until the buffer is full or EOF/error, so a short read of 10 would be followed by another read which fails with EIO → badbit. But raw `read()` returning a short count is NOT EOF for arbitrary fds — and here the injector deliberately returns a short read (10 bytes) to simulate partial delivery, expecting the caller to read again and get the error.

So my loop logic is wrong: with raw read(), a short read must not be treated as EOF. I must loop until read returns 0 (EOF) or -1 (error). For regular files, short reads only happen at EOF, but the injector (and theoretically other cases) can return short reads. Correct semantics: keep reading while got > 0; EOF only when got == 0.

But wait — the original code's comment says "A final read shorter than the chunk -- including zero bytes on an empty file -- after a non-failed read is normal EOF". With ifstream.read(), gcount() < size means EOF was reached (filebuf sets eofbit only when it hits EOF). So the equivalent with raw read is: got == 0 → EOF; got > 0 → consume and continue; got < 0 → error.

Let me fix the loop:

```cpp
while (true) {
    ssize_t got;
    do {
        got = read(file.fd, buffer.data(), buffer.size());
    } while (got < 0 && errno == EINTR);
    if (got > 0) {
        if (!sink.consume(...)) return false;
        continue;
    }
    if (got < 0) { ...read error... }
    break;  // got == 0: normal end of file
}
```

Also update comments: EOF is a zero-length read, not a short read.

